title: CHIP and CCUS
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

* ''Distinction:'' Both are clonal precursor states; neither meets criteria for a myeloid neoplasm.
* ''CHIP:'' Clonal hematopoiesis of indeterminate potential; myeloid-driver mutation, variant allele fraction (VAF) ≥2%.
** No unexplained cytopenia; implications include myeloid malignancy and cardiovascular risk.
** Confirm blood counts, differential, smear, and mutation pathogenicity; review prior cytotoxic exposure.
** Normal counts and low-risk features generally do not require marrow biopsy.
* ''CCUS:'' Clonal cytopenia of undetermined significance; clonality with persistent, otherwise unexplained cytopenia.
** Exclude nutritional deficiencies, medications, infection, renal disease, and other secondary causes.
** Obtain marrow aspirate/biopsy, cytogenetics, and myeloid sequencing to exclude myelodysplastic neoplasms (MDS).
** MDS-defining genetic abnormalities may establish MDS despite absent diagnostic dysplasia.
* ''Risk stratification:'' Use the clonal hematopoiesis risk score (CHRS) for either condition.
** Higher risk: cytopenia, multiple mutations, larger clones, spliceosome mutations, and abnormal red-cell indices.
** Isolated DNMT3A generally carries lower risk; CCUS progression risk remains heterogeneous.
* ''CHIP management:'' Reassure low-risk patients; individualize blood-count follow-up to risk and clinical change.
** Address smoking, hypertension, lipids, and diabetes; CHIP alone does not mandate aspirin.
* ''CCUS management:'' Observe; higher-risk cases need blood counts approximately every 3–6 months.
** Stable lower-risk cases may need less frequent surveillance; repeat marrow if counts worsen.
** No established preventive clone-directed therapy; consider clinical trials for high-risk CCUS.

Sources: ASH-SAP, 9th ed. (2025), ch. 40, pp. 485–487; NCCN Myelodysplastic Syndromes v3.2026 (January 12), MDS-2; Discussion MS-7–8; [[ICC 2022|https://doi.org/10.1182/blood.2022015850]]; [[Weeks 2023|https://doi.org/10.1056/EVIDoa2200310]]; [[ASH precursor conditions 2022|https://www.hematology.org/education/trainees/fellows/hematopoiesis/2022/myeloid-malignancy-precursor-conditions]].

